 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The worker uses the 2Q eviction algorithm to resist sequential screening scans while retaining repeatedly accessed comparison sheets:
+- **FIFO Queue ($A1$ / first-in / probationary)**: Newly rendered previews are admitted here upon first access, occupying up to a configured fraction of the 12 GiB capacity (e.g., 25% or $K_{in}$). If an entry reaches the tail of $A1$ without a second access, it is evicted.
+- **LRU Queue ($Am$ / main / hot)**: When a preview currently resident in $A1$ is requested again (or upon re-reference per 2Q design), it is promoted to the hot LRU queue ($Am$). Subsequent hits on entries in $Am$ move them to the head of $Am$.
+- When admitting a new preview into $A1$ or promoting an entry into $Am$ causes total cached JPEG data to exceed 12 GiB, space is reclaimed by evicting from the appropriate queue tail.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (Two-Queue) replacement | LRU, Frequency-based with aging | Protects frequently compared contact sheets from being evicted during long sequential screening scans by requiring a second reference before promotion to the main LRU queue ($Am$), keeping all bookkeeping private to the worker. |
+
